From 7be4f7e7862f4c81989cfe151a60aa2a793e3c8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:07:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] input: a key can leave the mouse pointer on screen

Every key of a keyboard, a remote, CEC or the event server hides the mouse
pointer, as does a button of the mouse that keymaps turn into another
action. A remote that drives the pointer and has keys too (a TV remote with
a pointer, through an add-on) then loses its pointer at each key.

Whatever drives the pointer can now ask it to stay with
Window(Home).Property(MousePointerStays): "true" for every key, "nomove"
for every key but up, down, left and right, which move the focus. The name
and the values are those of the YBLOD build, so an add-on made for it does
the same here; any case is taken. Unset, or any other value, nothing
changes: the mouse is turned off where it was, and all that is added is a
read of the property. A pointer that stays still is hidden after its time
without movement as before. kodi.log says the value when it changes.

Decision in MousePointerStays.h. Tests (outside the tree):
TestMousePointerStays (5 cases, every action id in each mode, 7 mutants
that it catches) and a check of the wiring that fails on the tree before.
In QEMU, with a mouse and a keyboard: unset, a key hides the pointer as
before (the same pixels as the build before); "true" keeps it at a key and
at down; "nomove" keeps it at a key and hides it at up. The idea is from
YBLOD (kodi-9994).
